WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.072
On Day 58, every Pod got its own Envoy.

2
00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.666
Istio calls that sidecar mode.

3
00:00:08.666 --> 00:00:12.384
It is the original design, used since 2017.

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.378
Every call passes through two of them, the caller's and the receiver's.

5
00:00:17.378 --> 00:00:22.021
Each proxy handles both L4 and L7, so every feature works everywhere.

6
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:24.358
But the cost grows with the mesh.

7
00:00:24.358 --> 00:00:27.900
Each sidecar is sized for its own Pod's busiest moment.

8
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:31.575
Add Pods, and you add Envoys, one for every Pod.

9
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.009
And joining the mesh, or upgrading it, means restarting every Pod.

10
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:39.983
Ambient mode splits that work into two layers.

11
00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:45.036
It became generally available in Istio 1.24, in November 2024.

12
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:49.252
The first layer is ztunnel, a small proxy written in Rust.

13
00:00:49.252 --> 00:00:52.000
It runs once per node, not once per Pod.

14
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:58.628
When a Pod joins, the Istio CNI node agent writes redirect rules inside the Pod's network.

15
00:00:58.628 --> 00:01:02.465
They send its traffic to the ztunnel on the same node.

16
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:09.872
ztunnel encrypts and authenticates the traffic of every Pod on its node, and enforces L4 rules.

17
00:01:09.872 --> 00:01:11.768
But it never parses HTTP.

18
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.444
Each ztunnel holds certificates only for the Pods on its own node.

19
00:01:16.444 --> 00:01:18.733
An app container never sees a key.

20
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:22.825
Follow one call, from client to hello-devops.

21
00:01:22.825 --> 00:01:25.721
The client opens a plain TCP connection.

22
00:01:25.721 --> 00:01:27.821
It has no idea a mesh exists.

23
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:36.749
ztunnel wraps the bytes in a tunnel called HBONE, short for HTTP-Based Overlay Network Environment.

24
00:01:36.749 --> 00:01:39.567
It is mutual TLS, on port 15008.

25
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.377
ztunnel presents the client's certificate.

26
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:46.427
So the other side learns who is calling from the handshake itself.

27
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:54.640
The ztunnel on the other node checks its L4 policy, unwraps the tunnel, and delivers plain TCP to the app.

28
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.711
That is the cheap path.

29
00:01:56.711 --> 00:02:02.005
Encryption, identity and L4 policy, with no HTTP parsing anywhere.

30
00:02:02.233 --> 00:02:04.143
But some features need HTTP.

31
00:02:04.143 --> 00:02:09.393
A canary split by weight, a match on a header, a rule about paths or methods.

32
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.589
For those, you add a waypoint.

33
00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:16.760
It is a full Envoy that runs as its own Deployment, usually one per namespace.

34
00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:23.395
Now ztunnel sends the bytes to the waypoint instead of straight to a Pod, still inside HBONE.

35
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.397
The waypoint parses HTTP.

36
00:02:25.397 --> 00:02:31.507
It applies the route split and the AuthorizationPolicy, and picks a hello-devops Pod.

37
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:37.607
Then it opens a new HBONE tunnel, to the ztunnel on that Pod's node, which delivers it.

38
00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:42.018
Most calls only need encryption and identity, which are cheap.

39
00:02:42.018 --> 00:02:45.932
So you pay for HTTP parsing only where you add a waypoint.

40
00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:53.617
Istio's page comparing the two modes quotes its latency tests, at p90 and p99.

41
00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:59.323
With sidecars, 0.63 to 0.88 milliseconds per call.

42
00:02:59.533 --> 00:03:03.935
With ztunnel alone, 0.16 to 0.20.

43
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:08.909
And through a waypoint, 0.40 to 0.50.

44
00:03:09.833 --> 00:03:14.442
To use ambient mode, install Istio with the ambient profile.

45
00:03:14.442 --> 00:03:18.743
It brings istiod, ztunnel, and the Istio CNI node agent.

46
00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:21.416
Then decide how each namespace joins.

47
00:03:21.416 --> 00:03:24.726
Say namespace hello-mesh already has Pods running.

48
00:03:24.726 --> 00:03:26.645
Here it is, joining each way.

49
00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:31.802
In sidecar mode you label the namespace istio-injection equals enabled.

50
00:03:31.802 --> 00:03:34.304
Nothing happens to the running Pods.

51
00:03:34.533 --> 00:03:39.489
Only Pods created after the label get a sidecar, so you restart your Deployments.

52
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.980
In ambient mode you label it istio.io/dataplane-mode equals ambient.

53
00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.396
ztunnel takes over the running Pods at once.

54
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.823
No restart, no extra container.

55
00:03:50.823 --> 00:03:58.064
The node agent adds redirect rules inside each running Pod's network, and the Pod spec never changes.

56
00:03:58.267 --> 00:04:07.361
When that namespace needs HTTP features, one istioctl command deploys a waypoint, and sends the namespace's traffic through it.

57
00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:08.224
One rule.

58
00:04:08.224 --> 00:04:11.072
Never put both labels on one namespace.

59
00:04:11.072 --> 00:04:14.285
If you do, sidecar mode wins for those Pods.

60
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.531
Sidecar mode puts an Envoy in every Pod.

61
00:04:18.531 --> 00:04:28.305
Ambient mode puts one ztunnel on each node, for L4 and mutual TLS over HBONE, and adds a waypoint only where you need L7.
